            <ArticleTitle>Differing reports of asthma symptoms in African Americans and Caucasians.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>165-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/02770900701847076</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This pilot study explores the reported symptoms in African Americans and Caucasians with asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Asthma patients in an inner-city pulmonary clinic were given a brief questionnaire of asthma symptoms and the BORG scale, followed by spirometry.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">African Americans were less likely to report nocturnal awakenings (67% vs. 100%; p = 0.037), complain of dyspnea (33% vs. 75%; p = 0.038), or experience chest pain (13% vs. 75%; p = 0.002) than Caucasians.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This is the first study to demonstrate that there are clinically significant differences in the reporting of asthma symptoms between African Americans and Caucasians.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
